WEBVTT

00:00:00.530 --> 00:00:03.550
Preparation happens before the alert that everyone remembers.

00:00:03.550 --> 00:00:07.600
It is the quiet work that makes the rest of incident response possible: assigning roles,

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.500
defining escalation paths, maintaining playbooks, confirming tool access,

00:00:11.500 --> 00:00:15.720
checking log availability, testing backups, and making sure decision makers can be

00:00:15.720 --> 00:00:16.090
reached.

00:00:16.090 --> 00:00:20.570
Preparation also includes legal, privacy, communications, and business-owner coordination,

00:00:20.570 --> 00:00:22.798
because many incidents are not purely technical.

00:00:22.798 --> 00:00:25.275
A suspicious login may affect an employee account,

00:00:25.275 --> 00:00:27.900
a cloud credential exposure may involve customer data,

00:00:27.900 --> 00:00:30.900
and a system outage may require business continuity decisions.

00:00:30.900 --> 00:00:34.095
The team should know who can approve containment, who can contact vendors,

00:00:34.095 --> 00:00:35.130
who can speak to leadership,

00:00:35.130 --> 00:00:36.640
and where evidence should be stored.

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:41.160
Tabletop exercises and drills are useful because they expose gaps before a real incident

00:00:41.160 --> 00:00:41.535
does.

00:00:41.535 --> 00:00:43.960
A playbook that has never been tested is only a theory.

00:00:43.960 --> 00:00:48.125
Readiness means people understand their role, monitoring provides useful context,

00:00:48.125 --> 00:00:49.780
backups can actually be restored,

00:00:49.780 --> 00:00:52.085
and communication paths work when time matters.

00:00:52.085 --> 00:00:55.050
Good preparation lowers confusion, shortens response time,

00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.621
and gives responders confidence that they can act safely.

00:00:57.621 --> 00:01:01.960
A practical readiness review should also check boring details that become critical later:

00:01:01.960 --> 00:01:03.545
who can approve emergency access,

00:01:03.545 --> 00:01:06.430
where bridge notes are kept, which logs are retained long enough,

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:08.390
and how the team reaches a vendor or cloud

00:01:08.390 --> 00:01:09.675
provider after hours.

00:01:09.675 --> 00:01:14.080
Preparation turns those details into known paths instead of last-minute discoveries.
